+# Agent Note: MCP server manager plugin
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-12-mcp-server-manager-plugin.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The [MCP client plugin](2026-07-07-mcp-client-plugin.md) bridges exactly one external MCP server per `cordis.yml` entry. Adding, removing, or disabling a server therefore means hand-editing a composition file and restarting the Host. Users who do not read YAML cannot manage MCP servers at all, there is no runtime view of which servers are connected, and the model itself cannot add a server even when the user asks for one.
+
+## Decision
+
+`packages/mcp/mcp-manager` (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager`) owns one persisted registry of MCP server definitions and mounts one `dsh-mcp-client` child fiber per enabled entry. The registry is the single source of truth for three surfaces: the Host mount loop, the model-facing management tools, and the browser settings card.
+
+**Registry.** The value is `{ servers: McpServerEntry[] }`, where an entry carries one transport selector (`stdio` or `streamable-http`) plus the union of both transports' fields. `validateServerEntry` enforces the cross-field rule the Schemastery schema cannot express per branch: a stdio entry needs a command, a streamable-http entry needs an absolute HTTP(S) URL. The `name` field is the `serverName` namespace `dsh-mcp-client` uses, so the public tool name `mcp__<name>__<tool>` stays stable across reloads.
+
+**Mount reconciliation.** `apply` installs the registry as a settings section and reconciles the registry against a map of live child fibers. `planReconcile` is a pure diff over `mountSignature` (a JSON projection of the client config): an unchanged signature keeps its live connection, a changed one stops and restarts, a new one starts, and a removed, disabled, duplicated, or invalid one stops. A child is started with `ctx.plugin(McpClient, config)` and `fiber.await()`; it is stopped with `fiber.dispose()`. Because the child is the real `dsh-mcp-client`, its tools register on the same `ctx.tools` runtime every other tool uses — the harness calls MCP tools natively, with no manager-side proxy. Children mount with `failOnStartupError: false` so a server that is down at startup keeps its reconnect supervisor instead of aborting the mount.
+
+**Model tools.** `mcp_manager_list`, `mcp_manager_add`, `mcp_manager_remove`, and `mcp_manager_set_enabled` delegate to one `ManagerPort` that the mount loop also uses. Every mutation goes through the settings section when one is composed, so a chat edit and a card edit cannot diverge, and the tool returns the fresh status table as JSON.
+
+**Browser page.** The client half registers its own `settings.section` entry with id `mcp-manager` and order 25, so it renders as the **MCP servers** page below **Agent presets** rather than a tab inside Plugins; the package therefore no longer depends on `ui-settings-plugins`. The page edits the same section through the client settings scope and reports each server's transport, target, enabled state, and connection status, listing servers five per page behind a name/target filter, with an add dialog carrying the transport-dependent form. The registry is opt-in per deployment: the package declares `dsh.bundle.patch` (`cordis.patch.yml`), so `dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager` appends one `mcp-manager` row to that profile's `dsh.profile.bundles`, while a one-off run can pass the CLI overlay (`apps/cli/config/examples/mcp-manager/mcp-manager.cordis.yml`) to `--patch`. No shipped profile includes it, and a deployment that never composes it shows no page. The user-facing install and Web-UI walkthrough is [Manage MCP servers from the Web UI](../../../../docs/user/guide/mcp-servers.md).
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Keep one `cordis.yml` entry per server.** This preserves the existing explicit composition, but it is exactly the manual YAML editing the feature exists to remove, and it cannot support runtime add/remove or a UI.
+
+**Store the registry in a new file beside `cordis.yml`.** A separate file would need its own read/write, validation, and reload path duplicated from the settings service, and would not appear in the settings card or participate in the existing revision fencing.
+
+**Wrap each MCP tool with a manager-owned proxy tool.** A proxy would have to re-implement schema translation, image admission, result projection, and per-call timeouts that `dsh-mcp-client` already owns, and would double the tool list. Mounting the real client keeps one bridge and one naming contract.
+
+**Let the model tools own connection lifetime directly.** Tool calls are request-scoped, so a connection opened inside one call would have no owner between calls. Reconciliation belongs to the plugin effect scope, where disposal is guaranteed.
+
+## Consequences
+
+MCP servers become first-class runtime state: they can be added, disabled, or removed from chat or the settings card, and the model can manage them without a Host restart. The manager inherits `dsh-mcp-client`'s naming, reconnect, and image-admission behavior unchanged, so a guarded server can still be reached after it comes back, and every existing mcp-client test keeps its meaning. The manager adds one indirection between configuration and the client: a change is applied on the next reconciliation tick rather than synchronously, so a just-added tool appears after the child fiber activates. Duplicate names resolve last-row-wins, matching every other configuration layer. Unit tests pin the schema, validation, projection, and pure diff; an integration suite pins mount, add, remove, and disable against a stubbed child; and an end-to-end suite drives a real stdio fixture server through the fixture's real MCP protocol and calls one of its tools.
+
+## Testing
+
+`packages/mcp/mcp-manager/tests` holds the package at 100% of statements, branches, functions, and lines under the repository's `perFile` thresholds. A unit suite pins the registry schema, validation, client projection, and the pure reconcile diff; an integration suite pins mount, add, remove, and disable against a child stubbed at the `dsh-mcp-client` package boundary; an end-to-end suite spawns the real stdio fixture server and calls `mcp__fixture__add` for a real `42`. The client half is covered by controller unit tests (draft parsing, filter matching, page clamping, and the add dialog's open/close), a jsdom render of the page (empty state, filter box, pager bounds, dialog, and gestures), a real `Context`/`SlotRegistry`/`LocaleRuntime` test of the plugin injection, and an artifact test that loads the built `lib/client.js` handoff and asserts the registered section id, order, locale label, and teardown. A stylesheet contract test pins every `var(--dsw-*)` name in the card to a declaration in `packages/client/ui-theme`, forbids literal colour fallbacks, and pins the primary action to the shared `--dsw-alias-button-primary-fill` / `--dsw-alias-label-primary-foreground` pair, so an undeclared name cannot leave the Add button invisible. Beyond the suite, the page was driven in a real browser against `dsh web --patch apps/cli/config/examples/mcp-manager/mcp-manager.cordis.yml`: the nav entry opened below Agent presets, the add dialog's form spawned the child MCP process and wrote the `mcp-manager` settings section, the filter and the pager narrowed the list, disabling the server disposed that process, and deleting it returned the section to `[]`. The Profile-bundle install path was reproduced against a scratch `DSH_HOME`: `dsh plugin --profile web add <package path>` appended the package to `dsh.profile.bundles` and put the `mcp-manager` row at the end of `dsh --profile web --dump-config`, and `dsh plugin --profile web remove` withdrew both. The four management tools are registered in `docs/tool-catalog.md` and its Chinese mirror, so their schemas are part of the documented tool surface; the `add` description keeps its `mcp__<name>__<tool>` placeholder inside backticks because the catalog renders description prose as Markdown, and a bare placeholder fails the documentation-site build.
