+# Agent Note: Skills manager plugin
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-13-skills-manager-plugin.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The [skill capability family](../../../../packages/skill/README.md) discovers skills from `SKILL.md` files in project, custom, and user directories. Adding, changing, disabling, or removing a skill therefore means writing files on disk, and a user who wants a skill only in this harness — or who is not working inside a repository at all — has no roster to edit. There is no runtime view of which skills the harness serves, no way to disable one without deleting the file, and no way for the model to add one when the user asks for it.
+
+## Decision
+
+`packages/skill/skills-manager` (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager`) owns one persisted registry of skill definitions and publishes it through a single `ctx.skills` provider. The registry is the single source of truth for three surfaces: the provider the harness reads, the model-facing management tools, and the browser settings card.
+
+**Registry.** The value is `{ skills: StoredSkill[] }`, where an entry mirrors the `SKILL.md` frontmatter a user would otherwise write (`name`, `description`, `whenToUse`, `content`) plus the manager's `disableModelInvocation`, `userInvocable`, and `enabled` flags. Field names follow the frontmatter keys on purpose, so a body written here loads through the same pipeline as a discovered one. `validateStoredSkill` enforces the cross-field rules the Schemastery schema cannot express: a kebab-case name, a non-empty description, a non-empty body, and the per-field length bounds.
+
+**Provider, not a proxy.** `apply` registers one `SkillProvider` synchronously, as the registry requires. The provider re-reads the mutable registry on every `list` and `get`, so an edit needs no re-registration — only `control.invalidate()`, which the manager calls after each write. Because it registers on the real seam, the shipped session catalog merges managed candidates with the filesystem ones and the shipped `skill` tool loads a managed body by re-entering this provider's `get`. The manager ranks candidates at `350`, so a repository's project roots (`100`/`200`) and `customSkillDirs` (`300`) win a duplicate name while managed skills still beat every user-level root (`400`/`500`) and bundled skills (`600`). A malformed entry is logged and skipped rather than failing the whole discovery.
+
+**Model tools.** `skills_manager_list`, `skills_manager_add`, `skills_manager_remove`, and `skills_manager_set_enabled` delegate to one port that the browser card also writes through, so a chat edit and a card edit cannot diverge, and each tool returns the fresh roster as JSON.
+
+**Browser page.** The client half registers its own `settings.section` entry with id `skills-manager` and order 26, so it renders as the **Skills** page below Agent presets (and below the MCP servers page where that is composed) rather than a tab inside Plugins. The page edits the same section through the client settings scope, lists skills five per page behind a name/description filter, and carries an add-or-edit dialog.
+
+**Opt-in per deployment.** The package declares `dsh.bundle.patch` (`cordis.patch.yml`), so `dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager` appends one `skills-manager` row to that profile's `dsh.profile.bundles`; a one-off run can pass the CLI overlay (`apps/cli/config/examples/skills-manager/skills-manager.cordis.yml`) to `--patch`. No shipped profile includes it, and a deployment that never composes it shows no page. The user-facing install and Web-UI walkthrough is [Manage agent skills from the Web UI](../../../../docs/user/guide/skills.md).
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Write `SKILL.md` files instead of a registry.** A manager-managed directory would have to own placement, naming, watching, and removal, duplicating the filesystem provider, and it still would not give the settings card a value to edit or the model a tool to call.
+
+**Register a private loader tool that returns skill bodies.** A manager-owned loader would re-implement the session catalog and re-do the `skill` tool's policy checks. Registering a provider instead makes a managed skill indistinguishable from a discovered one and reuses every existing consumer unchanged.
+
+**Store the registry in a new file beside the settings document.** A separate file would need its own read, write, validation, and reload path duplicated from the settings service, and it would not participate in the existing revision fencing.
+
+**Wrap the filesystem provider and filter its results.** A wrapper would have to re-derive every root, watcher, and priority rule the filesystem provider owns, and it would still have nowhere to store a skill that no file backs.
+
+**Let the model tools mutate the provider's cache directly.** Tool calls are request-scoped and the registry is durable state; a write outside the settings section would not survive a restart and would silently diverge from the card.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Skills become first-class runtime state: they can be added, edited, disabled, or removed from chat or the settings card without a Host restart. The manager inherits the skill registry's merge, invocation-policy, and catalog behavior unchanged, so a managed skill obeys the same flags and appears in the same `<available_skills>` catalog as a discovered one, and every existing skill test keeps its meaning. The manager adds one indirection between the document and the provider: a change is applied on the next discovery rather than synchronously, so a just-added skill appears in the catalog on the next model step. A duplicate name resolves by rank within one layer, so the manager deliberately cannot shadow a repository's own checked-in skill. Unit tests pin the schema, validation, and projection; integration tests pin provider registration, runtime edits, the settings section, and the harness's own `skill` tool over the managed registry; and a browser suite pins the card's controller, render, injection, artifact, and stylesheet contracts.
+
+## Testing
+
+`packages/skill/skills-manager/tests` holds the package at 100% of statements, branches, functions, and lines under the repository's `perFile` thresholds. A unit suite pins the registry schema, validation, invocation policy, and candidate and definition projection; a provider suite pins discovery, per-entry skip-and-warn, and load-after-edit; a manager suite pins provider registration, runtime edits, and the settings section against a real `Context`; and a tools suite pins each `skills_manager_*` schema and its JSON result. An integration suite mounts the real `SkillRegistry`, `tool-skill`, and `ToolRuntime` and asserts the harness's own `skill` tool returns a managed skill's body inside `<skill_content>`, so "the harness can call the skill" is pinned at the shipped boundary rather than mocked. The client half is covered by controller unit tests (draft parsing, validation, filter matching, paging, and the dialog), a jsdom render of the page (empty state, filter box, pager bounds, and gestures), a real `Context`/`SlotRegistry`/`LocaleRuntime` test of the plugin injection, an artifact test that loads the built `lib/client.js` handoff and asserts the registered section id, order, locale label, and teardown, and a stylesheet contract test that pins every `var(--dsw-*)` name in the card to a declaration in `packages/client/ui-theme`. Beyond the suite, the page was driven in a real browser against both a scratch `file:` install and a registry install of `@ctl456/dsh-skills-manager@latest`: the nav entry opened below Agent presets, the add dialog published a skill and wrote the `skills-manager` settings section, and removing it returned the section to `[]`. The four management tools are registered in `docs/tool-catalog.md` and its Chinese mirror, so their schemas are part of the documented tool surface.
