+# Manage agent skills from the Web UI
+
+English | [中文](skills.zh.md)
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager` is an opt-in bundle that keeps a list of agent skills and publishes them through the harness's own skill registry. You add, edit, disable, and remove skills from the Web settings page or from chat, and the harness loads each one with its built-in `skill` tool. No YAML editing, no `SKILL.md` files, and no host restart.
+
+## Install the bundle
+
+The bundle ships with the `dsh` release set. Install it into the profile you boot:
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile web add @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager
+dsh --profile web
+```
+
+Any profile works; `web` is the Web UI profile. `dsh plugin --profile web remove @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager` removes the row; the next start withdraws every skill it had published.
+
+From a source checkout, point the same command at the package directory instead, after `pnpm run build` has produced its client bundle:
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile web add link:/path/to/deepseek-harness/packages/skill/skills-manager
+```
+
+[Package and install a plugin](../develop/basic/publish.md) explains what makes a package installable as a bundle.
+
+## Manage skills in the Web UI
+
+Open **Settings → Skills** — its own page in the settings nav, below **Agent presets**.
+
+![The Skills page on first use: the filter box and Add skill button above an empty list](skills-card.png)
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| Name | The skill's identifier: lowercase letters, digits, and single hyphens, up to 64 characters. The model loads the skill by this exact name. |
+| Description | One line saying what the skill does and when to use it; the model routes on this text. |
+| When to use | Optional extra triggering guidance, for example "when the user asks for release notes". |
+| Instructions | The procedure in Markdown. Write the steps the model should follow after loading it. |
+| Let the model invoke it | When off, the skill is stored but stays out of the model's skill catalog. |
+| Show in the / menu | When off, the skill stays out of the composer's slash list. |
+
+**Add skill** opens a dialog. Fill the fields and press **Save**; the skill is published immediately, with no restart.
+
+![The add dialog with the name, description, when-to-use, instructions, and the two invocation switches](skills-dialog.png)
+
+The page lists every configured skill with its description, size, enabled state, and **Edit**, **Enable**/**Disable**, and **Remove** controls. A name that already exists replaces that entry. The list pages five skills at a time, and the filter box matches on both the name and the description.
+
+![The page after adding a skill: the row shows its description, size, and controls](skills-configured.png)
+
+## Manage skills from chat
+
+`skills_manager_list` reads the current list; `skills_manager_add`, `skills_manager_remove`, and `skills_manager_set_enabled` change it. Every call returns the fresh list with each skill's enabled state, invocation policy, body size, and configuration problems, so you can ask the model to add a skill instead of filling the form.
+
+## Where the configuration lives
+
+The card and the tools edit the same `skills-manager` section of `$DSH_HOME/settings.yaml`. The `skills` array in the composition row is the seeded base layer, so a profile or `--patch` overlay can preconfigure skills while the settings document stays the value that wins. A deployment that composes no settings provider keeps the composition list and the card reports that the manager is unavailable.
+
+## What happens to each skill
+
+The manager registers one ordinary skill provider, so every enabled, valid entry is merged with the skills discovered from disk and the harness's session catalog advertises the winners to the model. The `skill` tool loads a managed body exactly as it loads a `SKILL.md`, and adding, editing, or removing a skill takes effect on the next model step without restarting the host. A disabled, removed, or invalid entry stops appearing in the catalog; an invalid entry stays stored, with the problem reported from `skills_manager_list`.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- A managed name that collides with a repository's own skill loses: the filesystem provider's project roots outrank the manager, so a checked-in `SKILL.md` stays authoritative.
+- The card shows configuration, not whether the catalog currently serves an entry; the provider's skip warning goes to the host log, and the row's problem text is available through `skills_manager_list`.
+- Skill bodies are stored as plain settings values, so prefer instructions without secrets; the section is not a secret store.
+- The manager stores Markdown instructions only: it installs no scripts, no sandbox, and no extra tools.
+
+## Continue
+
