+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+`dsh-mcp-manager` is an opt-in Profile bundle that keeps a declarative list of external MCP servers and reconciles it against live `dsh-mcp-client` instances, so each enabled server's tools are callable as native tools. Install it with `dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager`; no shipped profile includes it. It adds runtime editing a static `cordis.yml` row cannot: the model uses management tools, and a person uses a settings page in the Web UI. Disabling or removing a server disposes its client; adding one mounts without a host restart. MCP resources and prompts remain unsupported.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+- [Dev Note](#dev-note)
+
+-----
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+### Install into a profile
+
+Install the package into the Profile that should manage MCP servers, then restart it. The package declares `dsh.bundle.patch`, so `dsh plugin` appends it to that profile's `dsh.profile.bundles` and the inserted row is live on the next start.
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager
+dsh plugin --profile <name> remove @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-manager
+dsh --profile <name>
+```
+
+A package without the `dsh.bundle.patch` declaration still installs, but only as a plain dependency: `dsh plugin` warns and the row never appears. Removing the package withdraws the row and every server it had mounted on the next start.
+
+### What you get
+
+One manager row; the manager mounts one client per enabled server itself, so a deployment no longer writes one `dsh-mcp-client` row per server. The inserted row's `servers` array seeds the composition base layer, and a profile overrides it by restating the `mcp-manager` row in its own patch. The user layer lives in the `mcp-manager` settings section (`$DSH_HOME/settings.yaml`), which the Web profile's settings provider persists; a deployment without a settings provider keeps the composition list and the card reports itself unavailable.
+
+Each entry carries both transports and defaults:
+
+| Field | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `name` | required | `serverName` namespace; tools appear as `mcp__<name>__<tool>` |
+| `transport` | required | `stdio` or `streamable-http` |
+| `enabled` | `true` | Whether the manager keeps a live client mounted |
+| `command` / `args` / `env` / `cwd` | — | stdio: executable, arguments, extra env, working directory |
+| `url` / `headers` | — | streamable-http: endpoint and extra request headers |
+| `toolCallTimeoutMs` | `60000` | Per-`tools/call` timeout |
+
+### Add a server from the Web UI
+
+Open **Settings → MCP servers**, the page below **Agent presets**. The page lists the configured servers, five per page, with a filter box that matches the name and the connection target. **Add server** opens a dialog: fill the name, choose the connection, and fill the fields that transport shows; press **Add**. Each listed server has an enable/disable switch and a remove button, and adding a name that already exists replaces that entry.
+
+### Add a server from chat
+
+Ask the model, or call the tools directly: `mcp_manager_list` reads the current list, `mcp_manager_add` adds or replaces one server, `mcp_manager_remove` deletes one, and `mcp_manager_set_enabled` toggles one without deleting it. Every mutation returns the fresh list with each server's tools and connection status.
+
+-----
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+The plugin owns one registry and two halves over it. The browser card writes the `servers` field through the revision-fenced settings scope; the model tools write the same field through `ctx.settings.update`. Both paths land in the section, whose watcher re-reads the resolved value and schedules reconciliation. Reconciliation calls the pure `planReconcile` diff: entries that are disabled, invalid, or duplicated never produce a client, an unchanged signature keeps the existing fiber, and a changed signature is stopped then started. `dsh-mcp-client` is mounted as a child fiber, so unloading the manager unmounts every server, and its tools register under the stable `mcp__<name>__` prefix the client already owns.
+
+### Source map
+
+| File | Role |
+|---|---|
+| [`src/servers.ts`](src/servers.ts) | Registry value shape, schema, validation, client projection, reconcile plan |
+| [`src/tools.ts`](src/tools.ts) | Model-facing `mcp_manager_*` tools over the registry port |
+| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry: settings section, mount loop, tool registration |
+| [`src/client/index.ts`](src/client/index.ts) | Browser half: settings section registration under the `mcp-manager` id |
+| [`src/client/card-controller.ts`](src/client/card-controller.ts) | Card form model over the settings scope |
+| — | No runtime invariant companion is published; reconciliation state lives in live child fibers and the settings document, so `mcp_manager_list` and the mount loop's tests own every observable relationship. |
+
