+---
+description: "The opt-in Profile bundle that adds, removes, enables, or disables agent skills from the Web settings page or from chat, publishing each one through ctx.skills so the harness loads it with its own skill tool."
+kind: "package-bundle"
+---
+
+# @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager
+
+English | [中文](README.zh.md)
+
+## Summary
+
+`dsh-skills-manager` is an opt-in Profile bundle that keeps a declarative list of agent skills and publishes it through one ordinary `ctx.skills` provider, so the harness's own session catalog advertises them and its own `skill` tool loads their bodies. Install it with `dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager`; no shipped profile includes it. It adds runtime editing a `SKILL.md` directory cannot: the model uses management tools, and a person uses a settings page in the Web UI. A skill added from chat or from the page is a first-class skill, not a private list entry.
+
+## Table of Contents
+
+- [Use this package](#use-this-package)
+- [Understand the implementation](#understand-the-implementation)
+- [Further Exploration](#further-exploration)
+- [Model Experience](#model-experience)
+- [Known Limitations and Deferred Work](#known-limitations-and-deferred-work)
+- [Dev Note](#dev-note)
+
+-----
+
+<a id="use-this-package"></a>
+## Use this package
+
+### Install into a profile
+
+Install the package into the Profile that should manage skills, then restart it. The package declares `dsh.bundle.patch`, so `dsh plugin` appends it to that profile's `dsh.profile.bundles` and the inserted row is live on the next start.
+
+```sh
+dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager
+dsh plugin --profile <name> remove @deepseek-ai/dsh-skills-manager
+dsh --profile <name>
+```
+
+A package without the `dsh.bundle.patch` declaration still installs, but only as a plain dependency: `dsh plugin` warns and the row never appears. Removing the package withdraws the row and every skill it had published on the next start.
+
+### What you get
+
+One manager row; the manager registers one `ctx.skills` provider itself, so a deployment no longer points the filesystem provider at a directory to get managed skills. The inserted row's `skills` array seeds the composition base layer, and a profile overrides it by restating the `skills-manager` row in its own patch. The user layer lives in the `skills-manager` settings section (`$DSH_HOME/settings.yaml`), which the Web profile's settings provider persists; a deployment without a settings provider keeps the composition list and the card reports itself unavailable.
+
+Candidates carry rank `350`, which resolves duplicates only within one layer. The shipped filesystem provider ranks a project's roots at `100`/`200` and `customSkillDirs` at `300`, and the user roots at `400`/`500` and bundled skills at `600`; `350` therefore keeps a repository's own skills authoritative while a managed skill still beats every user-level root.
+
+Each entry carries the `SKILL.md` frontmatter fields and one manager-only flag:
+
+| Field | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `name` | required | Kebab-case identifier; the model addresses the skill by this name |
+| `description` | required | One-line routing description shown in the session catalog |
+| `whenToUse` | — | Optional extra routing guidance |
+| `content` | required | Markdown instruction body the model reads after loading |
+| `disableModelInvocation` | `false` | When true, the skill is stored but hidden from the model catalog |
+| `userInvocable` | `true` | When false, the skill is hidden from the user's slash catalog |
+| `enabled` | `true` | Whether the provider publishes this entry |
+
+### Add a skill from the Web UI
+
+Open **Settings → Skills**, the page below **Agent presets** (and below **MCP servers** where that page is composed). The page lists the configured skills, five per page, with a filter box that matches the name and the description. **Add skill** opens a dialog: fill the name, the description, the optional when-to-use note, and the Markdown instructions, and set the two invocation switches; press **Save**. Each listed skill has **Edit**, an enable/disable switch, and **Remove**, and saving a name that already exists replaces that entry.
+
+### Add a skill from chat
+
+Ask the model, or call the tools directly: `skills_manager_list` reads the current list, `skills_manager_add` adds or replaces one skill, `skills_manager_remove` deletes one, and `skills_manager_set_enabled` toggles one without deleting it. Every mutation returns the fresh list with each skill's enabled state, invocation policy, size, and configuration problems.
+
+-----
+
+<a id="understand-the-implementation"></a>
+## Understand the implementation
+
+The plugin owns one registry and two halves over it. The browser card writes the `skills` field through the revision-fenced settings scope; the model tools write the same field through the same port. Both paths land in the section, whose watcher re-reads the resolved value and syncs the cached registry.
+
+Making a skill loadable is deliberately not this plugin's job. `apply` registers exactly one `SkillProvider` synchronously, as the registry requires, and the provider re-reads the mutable registry on every discovery and every load — so a change needs no re-registration, only a catalog invalidation, which the manager calls after each write. The registry merges these candidates with every other provider's under the rank above, the shipped session catalog renders the winners' names and descriptions, and the shipped `skill` tool resolves the body by re-entering this provider's `get`. One module owns the value shape because the settings section, the tools, the card, and the provider must all agree on it.
+
+### Source map
+
+| File | Role |
+|---|---|
+| [`src/skills.ts`](src/skills.ts) | Registry value shape, Schemastery schema, cross-field validation, provider projection |
+| [`src/provider.ts`](src/provider.ts) | The `ctx.skills` provider: discovery, load, and per-entry skip-and-warn |
+| [`src/tools.ts`](src/tools.ts) | Model-facing `skills_manager_*` tools over the registry port |
+| [`src/index.ts`](src/index.ts) | Plugin entry: settings section, provider registration, tool registration |
+| [`src/client/index.ts`](src/client/index.ts) | Browser half: settings section registration under the `skills-manager` id |
+| [`src/client/card-controller.ts`](src/client/card-controller.ts) | Card form model, draft validation, filter, and paging over the settings scope |
+| — | No runtime invariant companion is published; publish state lives in the settings document and the live provider, so the provider and tool tests own every observable relationship. |
+
+-----
+
+<a id="further-exploration"></a>
+## Further Exploration
+
+- [`dsh-skill`](../skill/README.md) — the registry and provider contract this manager registers on.
+- [`dsh-tool-skill`](../tool-skill/README.md) — the session catalog and the `skill` tool that loads a managed skill's body.
+- [Adding a settings card](../../../docs/cookbook/adding-a-settings-card.md) — the Host/browser pairing this card follows.
+- [skill/ — capability family](../README.md) — the package group this manager extends.
+
+-----
+
+<a id="model-experience"></a>
+## Model Experience
+
+### Management tools
+
+#### What the model sees
+
+Four small schemas: `skills_manager_list`, `skills_manager_add`, `skills_manager_remove`, `skills_manager_set_enabled`. Each returns pretty-printed JSON: the skill roster with enabled state, invocation policy, body size, and configuration problems. Every management call changes which skills the session catalog advertises on the next request; the `skill` tool's own schema is unchanged.
+
+#### Token effect
+
+Fixed schema cost on every request in that tool view, plus the session catalog's per-skill name and description — the reason skills are opt-in per entry and the catalog description is bounded.
+
+#### KV Cache effect
+
+An unchanged skill roster is prefix-stable. Adding, removing, or disabling a skill rewrites the catalog message rather than the tool definitions, so the first changed token is in that replacement reminder, not in the tool schema block.
+
+## Known Limitations and Deferred Work
+
+- **The page shows configuration, not whether the catalog currently serves it** — a skipped entry's problem text is available through `skills_manager_list` and the card's per-row diagnostics; the provider's own skip warning goes to the Host log.
+- **Skill bodies are stored as plain settings values** — the section is not a secret store, so do not paste credentials into instructions you expect to keep private.
+- **Invalid entries are stored, not refused at write time** — schema-valid but unusable entries (an empty description or body, a non-kebab-case name, an over-long field) are skipped by the provider and reported in `problems`.
+- **A managed skill cannot shadow a repository's own skill** — rank `350` loses to project roots on purpose, so a checked-in `SKILL.md` stays authoritative.
+- **Skills are instructions, not code** — the manager stores Markdown text; it adds no sandbox, no scripts, and no tool bundling beyond what any other skill gets.
+
+<a id="dev-note"></a>
+### Dev Note
+
+<details>
+<summary>Working context for maintainers — click to expand</summary>
+
+This Dev Note is working context for maintainers and explicitly non-authoritative — shipped behavior and limits live in the sections above and in the package code.
+
+- **The page was verified in a live browser, not only in jsdom** — both a scratch `file:` install and a registry install booted the `web` profile, rendered **Settings → Skills** below Agent presets, added a skill through the dialog, persisted it to the `skills-manager` section of `$DSH_HOME/settings.yaml`, and removed it again.
+- **The harness's own `skill` tool loads a managed skill** — an integration test mounts the real `SkillRegistry`, `tool-skill`, and `ToolRuntime` and asserts the `skill` tool returns the skill body, so "the harness can call the skill" is pinned at the shipped boundary rather than mocked.
+- **The four management tools are catalogued** — they are registered in `docs/tool-catalog.md` and its Chinese mirror, so an edit to a tool description changes a generated document; keep angle-bracket placeholders inside backticks, because a bare placeholder fails the documentation-site build.
+
+</details>
